course: dictionary
lesson: 672
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and bald is one of them. Read the words around it to find out which meaning the writer means. «My grandad is bald» is about hair: bald means having little or no hair on the head.
explain: «The tyres were bald, so the car slid on the wet road.» The clues are tyres, car and wet road, so bald must mean worn smooth, with no grip left. Always ask: what would fit here? Try the hair meaning in the sentence and it does not make sense.
explain: Bald can also mean plain, with no extra detail. Balding means losing hair. «Thinning on top» is a polite way to say going bald, so the polite phrase is the clue.
fact: Bald means having little or no hair on the head.
fact: «The bald eagle has a white-feathered head»; its name comes from an old word, balde, meaning white, so it is not really bald at all.
fact: Young bald eagles are brown all over until about five years old.
fact: Bald tyres have their grooves worn smooth. They grip poorly and are dangerous in the rain, because tyre treads push water away.
fact: Bald can also mean plain, with no extra detail, as in a bald statement or a bald fact.
exercise: «A vulture lands last, bald and scruffy, but it is one of nature's cleaners.» What does bald describe here? | Clean and shiny | Covered in water | *Having little or no hair on the head | Very loud
exercise: «A layer of water makes a car skid, so worn-smooth bald tyres are dangerous in the rain.» What does bald mean here? | Very long | Full of air | Brand new and shiny | *Worn smooth, with the grooves gone
exercise: «The bald eagle is not really bald.» Where does its name come from? | *An old word, balde, meaning white | A bird that has lost all its feathers | Its brown feathers | A word for a big nest
test: Tom says, «The report gave a bald fact and nothing more.» What does bald mean here? | Shiny and new | Worn smooth | *Plain, with no extra detail | Having no hair
test: What word means losing hair? | Bald tyres | Greying | Eagle | *Balding
exam: «Thinning on top» is a polite way to say what? | Losing a tooth | Growing a beard | *Going bald | Getting taller
